{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "What is the objective of this analysis — are we maximizing Carelland's total export revenue (i.e., the value of goods produced for the world market at the given Klunz prices), or is there another goal such as maximizing net trade balance or minimizing imports?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the optimization objective, presenting alternatives within one coherent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "When you say maximizing domestic GDP, do you mean maximizing the total value of all four goods produced (steel, engines, electronics, plastic) at their world-market prices, or is GDP defined differently here — for example, as the value of production minus the value of imported goods used as inputs?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy/definition choice regarding how GDP is calculated, presenting two mutually exclusive options within one sentence."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "is \"electronic components\" the same product as \"electronics\" (i.e., the third good produced), so that all four produced goods have a stated price?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the identity of two product names to confirm price availability."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The input table says producing one unit of steel requires 0.02 engines, one unit of engines requires 0.15 electronic components, and one unit of electronics requires 0.01 engines — do these mean that producing each good consumes a fraction of another good as an input (i.e., the goods are both produced and used up in production), or do they represent something else like a capital coefficient?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the interpretation of the input coefficients, presenting two alternative meanings for the user to choose from."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Since producing each good consumes fractions of other goods as inputs, is the quantity of each good available for export equal to total production minus the amount consumed as inputs in producing all four goods, or is there a separate export decision independent of domestic input use?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response presents a single clarification question asking for a specific policy choice between two mutually exclusive definitions of export availability."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "does this labor constraint apply only to the direct labor listed in the input table (6 person-months for steel, 1 person-year for engines, 6 person-months for electronics, 2 person-years for plastic), or does it also include labor embodied in the imported goods?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the scope of the labor constraint (direct vs. embodied/imported), which constitutes one independent decision point."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "is there a limit on the total amount of imported goods Carelland can bring in, or is the only constraint on imports the labor and production caps already stated?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the constraints on imported goods, presenting a binary choice between a specific limit and the existing caps."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "are steel and electronics production unlimited (subject only to the labor and input-availability constraints), or are there additional caps I should know about?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy clarification regarding the existence of production caps for steel and electronics, presenting a binary choice between unlimited production and additional caps."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "is the \"production\" figure for each good the gross output before subtracting the amount consumed as inputs elsewhere, or is it the net amount available after internal consumption?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the definition of the production figure (gross vs. net), which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "when I set up the model, should I treat these as simultaneous (i.e., the engines used in steel production come from the same engine output that also uses steel, so the system is solved jointly), or should I treat them as sequential (e.g., engines are produced first, then steel uses some of those engines)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice between two modeling approaches (simultaneous vs. sequential), which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The input table lists \"Klunz imported goods\" as an input for each product (250 for steel, 300 for engines, 50 for electronics, 300 for plastic) — are these numbers in Klunz (the currency unit), meaning they represent a cost that reduces GDP, or are they physical quantities of imported goods with no direct effect on the objective beyond consuming resources?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for the interpretation of the 'Klunz imported goods' values (currency cost vs. physical quantity), which constitutes one semantically independent request."
